## Problem

I needed one place to send people from freelance marketplaces and LinkedIn. It had to explain what I offer quickly, load fast on a phone, be easy for search engines and AI answer engines to read, and let me add posts and projects without editing code.

## Approach

- **Static by default.** Every page is generated at build time from content in the repo, so there's no database to run or pay for.
- **A git-based CMS.** Services, projects, posts, and site settings are edited in a Keystatic admin UI. Changes are saved as files in the repo, so content has the same history and review flow as code.
- **Technical SEO.** Per-page titles, descriptions, and canonical URLs; generated social share images; JSON-LD for the person, services, posts, and breadcrumbs; a sitemap built from the content; and an RSS feed.
- **Very little JavaScript.** The only client component is the mobile menu toggle. Fonts load through `next/font`, so text doesn't jump around while the page loads.
- **Cheap hosting.** It runs on Cloudflare Workers through the OpenNext adapter, on a free plan that allows commercial use.

## Stack

Next.js (App Router), TypeScript, Keystatic, Markdoc, Tailwind CSS, and Cloudflare Workers.

## Outcome

A site that I can update in a few minutes and that a client can inspect as a working example of the "fast, SEO-ready Next.js website" service.

Lighthouse scores, measured on a local production build:

- **Mobile:** Performance 96 to 99, Accessibility 100, Best Practices 100, SEO 100
- **Desktop:** 100 in all four categories
